.TH ASCII 1
.SH NAME
ascii \- print ASCII/character-encoding names
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ascii
[
.B -8cnt
] [
.BR -d | -o | -x " | " -b " n"
] [
.I text
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Ascii
is a real, structural translation of Plan 9's own
.IR ascii (1)
(from
.BR sys/src/cmd/ascii.c ),
compiled to Dis and run by
.IR emu ,
not a host binary. It lives under
.B p9/cmd
rather than
.BR appl/cmd ;
see
.B p9/dis/README
for how to bind it in.
.PP
With no arguments,
.I ascii
prints a table of the first 128 (or, with
.BR -8 ,
256) character codes, each row showing the code (in the current base,
default hexadecimal) and the character's name or printable form, eight
per line.
.PP
With one or more
.I text
arguments,
.I ascii
either prints the numeric code of each character in
.I text
.RB ( -n ,
or when
.I text
does not parse as a number in the current base), or treats
.I text
itself as a number (in the current base) and prints the NAME of that
character code
.RB ( -c ,
or when
.I text
DOES parse as a number and neither
.B -n
nor
.B -c
was given).
.B -t
is like
.B -c
but prints the raw character byte instead of its name.
.SS Options
.TP
.B -8
Use 256 characters (extended/Latin-1) instead of 128 (ASCII only).
.TP
.BR -x / -o / -d
Print codes in hexadecimal (default) / octal / decimal.
.TP
.BI -b " n"
Print codes in base
.I n
(2 through 36).
.TP
.B -n
Force numeric-code-of-each-character mode, even if
.I text
looks like a number.
.TP
.B -c
Force treat-argument-as-a-code, print-its-name mode.
.TP
.B -t
Like
.BR -c ,
but print the raw byte instead of the name.
.PP
All boolean flags may be bundled in one word
.RB ( "ascii -8x" " is the same as " "ascii -8 -x" ),
confirmed directly against the real binary before writing the option
parser, not assumed from the source's own
.B ARGBEGIN
macro expansion.
.SH SOURCE
.B /p9/cmd/ascii.b
(translated from
.BR sys/src/cmd/ascii.c ;
see its own header comment for the full derivation)
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR asciitest (1)
.SH BUGS
Diffed against plan9port's own copy before translating: identical
except ONE table entry - 9front labels character 0x0c (form feed)
.BR "ff " ;
plan9port labels it
.BR "np " " (new page)."
This translates 9front's own label, matching the repository's stated
primary target; plan9port's real
.B ascii
binary (used as the live oracle in
.IR asciitest (1),
since it is the copy actually installed on this host) will print
.B "np "
on that one row where this port prints
.BR "ff " " -"
a confirmed, understood divergence from the ORACLE, not from 9front's
own source, which is what is actually being translated.
.PP
Output is byte-, not rune-, based throughout: the
.B -t
flag writes a raw byte (0-255, including ones that are not valid
standalone UTF-8), and a
.I text
argument is read byte-by-byte, not rune-by-rune, matching the C's own
.RB ( "*s++&0377" )
byte-wise iteration. See
.BR ascii.b 's
own header comment for two real translation bugs this caught (the
.B -t
path emitting a rune's UTF-8 encoding instead of the one raw byte;
.B -8n
counting one code per rune instead of one per byte for a non-ASCII
argument), both found only once tests exercising a code above 127
were added.
